    <p>Beyond two-electron systems, many-body quantum mechanics requires exact anticommuting operator algebra and rigorous operator lower bounds:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Canonical Anticommutation Relations (<code>chem/fermiclaim.cub</code>, <code>fock.cub</code>, <code>pauli.cub</code>):</strong> Fermionic creation and annihilation operators satisfy \(a_i a_j^\dagger + a_j^\dagger a_i = \delta_{ij}\) and \(a_i^2 = 0\) as checked algebraic identities in Fock space (<code>fc-car</code>), directly encoding the Pauli exclusion principle.</li>
      <li><strong>Universal Polynomial Sum-of-Squares (<code>chem/algebra.cub</code>, <code>sosclaim.cub</code>):</strong> Polynomial identities over \(\mathbb{Q}\) are decided in canonical normal form (<code>al-id</code>), so any Sum-of-Squares positivity certificate holds simultaneously in every commutative ring containing \(\mathbb{Q}\) — the exact engine also reused by <code>neuro/lyapunov.cub</code> and <code>neuro/barrier.cub</code> for BCI stability proofs.</li>
      <li><strong>IMS Geometric Localization (<code>chem/cut.cub</code>):</strong> Formalizes the Ismagilov–Morgan–Simon partition-of-unity localization formula splitting a global kinetic Hamiltonian into local region Hamiltonians plus an exact gradient localization error \(\sum_k |\nabla \chi_k|^2\).</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="s5">
    <div class="sec-head">
      <h2>5. Chemical Identity: Resolving Cheminformatics' Entity-Resolution Crisis</h2>
      <span class="mod-tag">docs/chemical-identity.md · chem/</span>
    </div>
    <p>Pharmaceutical R&amp;D spends roughly $185 billion per year across the top 25 companies while fighting Eroom's Law (output per R&amp;D dollar halving every nine years). Sitting underneath every drug-discovery database is a broken primitive: <strong>chemical entity resolution</strong> — deciding when two records refer to the same substance.</p>
    <p>Standard InChI collapses three distinct mathematical relations into string comparison of a single canonical text string:</p>
